In article <xxspwkapj7e.fsf@tiswat.uto.dec.com>, han@uto.dec.com (Han Pilmeyer) writes:
|> Currently the C-compiler comes bundled with Digital UNIX. However,
|> ... to use for instance dbx one needs a development (OSF-DEV) license.

Wrong.  While the C compiler ships on the same media as the operating system, and
installs with the operating system, the base OS license only allows the compiler
to be used to rebuild and configure the kernel.  Unfortunately, the only
mechanism enforcing this license is in the dbx and DECladebug debuggers, thus
leading to the common misconception above.

It is entirely likely that the enforcement mechanisms and other packaging will 
change in the future, as new kernel development makes the use of the C compiler 
for tailoring and installation less necessary.

|> All opinions are those of the author, not of Digital Equipment!
