X/*
X * Internal structure of universal unique IDs (UUIDs).
X *
X * There are three "variants" of UUIDs that this code knows about.  The
X * variant #0 is what was defined in the 1989 HP/Apollo Network Computing
X * Architecture (NCA) specification and implemented in NCS 1.x and DECrpc
X * v1.  Variant #1 is what was defined for the joint HP/DEC specification
X * for the OSF (in DEC's "UID Architecture Functional Specification Version
X * X1.0.4") and implemented in NCS 2.0, DECrpc v2, and OSF 1.0 DCE RPC.
X * Variant #2 is defined by Microsoft.
X *
X * This code creates only variant #1 UUIDs.
X * 
X * The three UUID variants can exist on the same wire because they have
X * distinct values in the 3 MSB bits of octet 8 (see table below).  Do
X * NOT confuse the version number with these 3 bits.  (Note the distinct
X * use of the terms "version" and "variant".) Variant #0 had no version
X * field in it.  Changes to variant #1 (should any ever need to be made)
X * can be accomodated using the current form's 4 bit version field.
X * 
X * The UUID record structure MUST NOT contain padding between fields.
X * The total size = 128 bits.
X *
X * To minimize confusion about bit assignment within octets, the UUID
X * record definition is defined only in terms of fields that are integral
X * numbers of octets.
X *
X * Depending on the network data representation, the multi-octet unsigned
X * integer fields are subject to byte swapping when communicated between
X * dissimilar endian machines.  Note that all three UUID variants have
X * the same record structure; this allows this byte swapping to occur.
X * (The ways in which the contents of the fields are generated can and
X * do vary.)
X *
X * The following information applies to variant #1 UUIDs:
X *
X * The lowest addressed octet contains the global/local bit and the
X * unicast/multicast bit, and is the first octet of the address transmitted
X * on an 802.3 LAN.
X *
X * The adjusted time stamp is split into three fields, and the clockSeq
X * is split into two fields.
X *
X * |<------------------------- 32 bits -------------------------->|
X *
X * +--------------------------------------------------------------+
X * |                     low 32 bits of time                      |  0-3  .time_low
X * +-------------------------------+-------------------------------
X * |     mid 16 bits of time       |  4-5               .time_mid
X * +-------+-----------------------+
X * | vers. |   hi 12 bits of time  |  6-7               .time_hi_and_version
X * +-------+-------+---------------+
X * |Res|  clkSeqHi |  8                                 .clock_seq_hi_and_reserved
X * +---------------+
X * |   clkSeqLow   |  9                                 .clock_seq_low
X * +---------------+----------...-----+
X * |            node ID               |  8-16           .node
X * +--------------------------...-----+
X *
X * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
X *
X * The structure layout of all three UUID variants is fixed for all time.
X * I.e., the layout consists of a 32 bit int, 2 16 bit ints, and 8 8
X * bit ints.  The current form version field does NOT determine/affect
X * the layout.  This enables us to do certain operations safely on the
X * variants of UUIDs without regard to variant; this increases the utility
X * of this code even as the version number changes (i.e., this code does
X * NOT need to check the version field).
X *
X * The "Res" field in the octet #8 is the so-called "reserved" bit-field
X * and determines whether or not the uuid is a old, current or other
X * UUID as follows:
X *
X *      MS-bit  2MS-bit  3MS-bit      Variant
X *      ---------------------------------------------
X *         0       x        x       0 (NCS 1.5)
X *         1       0        x       1 (DCE 1.0 RPC)
X *         1       1        0       2 (Microsoft)
X *         1       1        1       unspecified
X *
X * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
X *
X * Internal structure of variant #0 UUIDs
X *
X * The first 6 octets are the number of 4 usec units of time that have
X * passed since 1/1/80 0000 GMT.  The next 2 octets are reserved for
X * future use.  The next octet is an address family.  The next 7 octets
X * are a host ID in the form allowed by the specified address family.
X *
X * Note that while the family field (octet 8) was originally conceived
X * of as being able to hold values in the range [0..255], only [0..13]
X * were ever used.  Thus, the 2 MSB of this field are always 0 and are
X * used to distinguish old and current UUID forms.
X *
X * +--------------------------------------------------------------+
X * |                    high 32 bits of time                      |  0-3  .time_high
X * +-------------------------------+-------------------------------
X * |     low 16 bits of time       |  4-5               .time_low
X * +-------+-----------------------+
X * |         reserved              |  6-7               .reserved
X * +---------------+---------------+
X * |    family     |   8                                .family
X * +---------------+----------...-----+
X * |            node ID               |  9-16           .node
X * +--------------------------...-----+
X *
X */
