organization: University of Missouri - Columbia
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc

Gopi K. Attaluri (gkattalu@neumann.uwaterloo.ca) wrote:

: hi,
:  I am writing RPC-based server application using Encina and DCE. I 
: run into problems such as corrupted stacks and corrupted pthread internal 
: data structures. I suspect that they are due to stack overflow of server 
: threads.

:  The documentation for pthreads says that stack-size must be specified 
: at thread creation time. But, the server threads are created by the RPC-runtime
: not the application itself. Is there any way I can "instruct" the RPC run-time 
: to allocate a larger stack to each thread? 

: Thanks,
: -gopi krishna.

: ---
: Gopi K. Attaluri
: Department of Computer Science
: University of Waterloo
: gkattalu@neumann.uwaterloo.ca




use rpc_mgmt_set_server_stack_size

#define STACKSIZE  (64*1024)
{
	int   st ;
	unsigned int  thread_stk_size = STACKSIZE ;

	rpc_mgmt_set_server_stack_size(thread_stk_size,&st) ;
	if (st != rpc_s_ok) {
		handle the error here 
	}
	thread_status = pthread_attr_getstacksize(thread_atribs) ;
 	CHECK(thread_status,"attr_getstacksize failed") ;
 	thread_status = pthread_attr_setstacksize(&thread_atribs,thread_stk_size);
 	CHECK(thread_status,"setstacksize failed");
} 

Ideally one would check if the current stack size is less than what is to be
set and then proceed to increase the stack size. 
hope this helps
-sirish

